{"id": "bc8f3e1a-c8dc-4c26-a854-c1dd79a87e58", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This EBS review is a broad account-wide survey rather than a root-cause hunt, so it's token-inefficient to do myself — better to delegate to a background sub-agent. Since the data mostly comes from the same source (EC2 API, maybe CloudWatch metrics), I'll keep it to a single focused sub-agent rather than parallelizing.

Actually, I'll split into two sub-agents since config review (volumes, snapshots, encryption, DLM/Backup) and metrics (burst balance, queue length, alarms) pull from different data sources — EC2 API vs CloudWatch. I should have them check list_associations to find all relevant accounts beyond the agent space account, and preload the exploring-metrics skill for the metrics agent while keeping the config agent generic.

"}]}

{"id": "59c1a6f1-4671-4f9e-a008-163a0f63932a", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll review your EBS setup in us-east-1 from two angles: configuration and protection, and performance and monitoring. Then I'll tell you whether it looks production-ready.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_u6OYd9Vjgy0SyjouctkVT0", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-config-review", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Production-readiness review of EBS configuration and data protection in us-east-1. Return a concise findings list with severity (High/Medium/Low) and concrete resource IDs.",
  "context": "User has a launch next week and wants to know whether EBS storage is production-ready. Cover the agent space account 012345678901_1 and any other associated AWS accounts (use list_associations to find them). Read-only calls only. Another reviewer covers CloudWatch performance metrics and alarms, so don't duplicate that.",
  "resources": ["EBS volumes, snapshots, and AMIs in us-east-1", "AWS Backup plans and DLM lifecycle policies in us-east-1"],
  "timeframe": "Current state, plus snapshot recency over the last 30 days",
  "criteria": "Check: (1) Unencrypted volumes and snapshots, and whether account-level EBS encryption by default is on, and which KMS keys are used. (2) Volumes with no recent snapshot, or not covered by a DLM policy or AWS Backup plan. Report the most recent snapshot age per attached production-looking volume. (3) Unattached (available) volumes, orphaned snapshots, and cost waste. (4) Volume types: gp2 volumes that could move to gp3, io1/io2 provisioning sanity, and undersized IOPS/throughput. (5) DeleteOnTermination on root and data volumes of instances. (6) Volumes in a single AZ with no replication or cross-region snapshot copy. (7) Public or widely shared snapshots. (8) Volumes in impaired or error state, and EBS service quota headroom (volumes, snapshots, storage TiB) via service-quotas. Group the output by theme and end with a short prioritized list of what to fix before launch."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review EBS configuration and backups in us-east-1"}, {"id": "tooluse_3pFKsf4azk1HbsNyEzYXMQ", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-performance-review", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Review EBS performance headroom and monitoring coverage in us-east-1 for production readiness. Return concise findings with severity (High/Medium/Low) and concrete volume IDs.",
  "context": "User has a launch next week and wants to know whether EBS storage is production-ready. Cover the agent space account 012345678901_1 and any other associated AWS accounts (use list_associations to find them). Read-only calls only. Another reviewer covers encryption, snapshots, backups and volume hygiene, so don't duplicate that.",
  "resources": ["EBS volumes attached to in-use instances in us-east-1", "CloudWatch AWS/EBS metrics and alarms in us-east-1"],
  "timeframe": "Last 14 days up to 2026-11-23T17:00:00Z",
  "criteria": "Check: (1) Burst balance on gp2, st1 and sc1 volumes, flagging any that run low. (2) Volumes near their IOPS or throughput limits (VolumeReadOps/WriteOps, VolumeReadBytes/WriteBytes against provisioned limits), and the VolumeQueueLength and VolumeIdleTime trends. (3) Instance-level EBS throughput or IOPS ceilings being hit, if visible in AWS/EC2 EBSReadOps, EBSWriteOps or EBSIOBalance%. (4) Whether CloudWatch alarms exist for EBS health, such as burst balance, queue length, StatusCheckFailed or volume stalled IO, and for EC2 status checks on instances with attached volumes. List the gaps. (5) Any volume status checks that are impaired or show io-enabled problems. (6) Pending or in-progress modify-volume operations. Finish with a short prioritized list of what to fix before launch."
}", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics"]}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review EBS performance headroom and alarms in us-east-1"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "5413ba67-5222-46ec-a197-9bef948ed934", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Since the DLM call got blocked as mutative, I should infer lifecycle policies from snapshot tags instead and check backup plan details via get_backup_plan, list_backup_selections, and list_recovery_points_by_resource. I also want to look at quotas, snapshot public sharing settings, default KMS keys for EBS, and whether any regional copy or backup copy actions exist.

I'm noticing an unencrypted volume named "kms-root" and an orphaned snapshot, while the 2025 AMI-backed snapshots aren't actually orphans since they're tied to AMIs rather than live volumes. Now I'm running through backup plan lookups, vault listings, service quota checks, default KMS key retrieval, volume modification history, and snapshot attribute checks (createVolumePermission) to see if any snapshots are publicly restorable, plus checking DLM tags and key details via KMS describe_key.

"}], "parent_id": "907cf79b-e4c5-42fd-a21b-4461106caf68"}

{"id": "0e29c933-fe77-4745-b690-dd2dfbc166a3", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm flagging that the KMS key backing an encrypted volume is disabled, which blocks starting or attaching it to the stopped instance—a high severity issue. I'm also noting the "Trial" backup plan has no selections so it protects nothing despite a recent execution, and the EBS quota list looks truncated, so I need to pull quotas for the relevant service code to check gp3/gp2 storage limits.

I'll check specific EBS quota codes for gp2, gp3, io1, st1, and sc1 storage, since volume count itself isn't an EBS quota. I should also see if DLM lifecycle policy calls are accessible despite being blocked earlier, and otherwise treat cross-region snapshot copies as out of scope.

"}], "parent_id": "907cf79b-e4c5-42fd-a21b-4461106caf68"}

{"id": "35398d73-0e8d-4dd8-8a24-e3a881d5229c", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The EBSIOBalance% on i-0cf8's t3.micro has hit zero, meaning it fully depleted its IOPS credits — the volume is provisioned for 16000 IOPS but the instance itself caps at 11800 max/500 baseline, so this is a real bottleneck. I want to pin down exactly when it dropped to zero and pull the queue length timeline to see when it dipped below 10.

I also notice the "example-burst-credits-low" alarm is actually attached to i-1234567890abcdef0_17, the healthy instance at 99%, not to the struggling i-0cf8 — that seems like a misconfigured alarm. And checking the math, the 32GB gp3 volume at 500 IOPS/GiB does correctly work out to 16000 IOPS provisioned, so that part's fine.

"}], "parent_id": "a8b60179-ad75-4091-a755-136e494e4f1e"}

{"id": "b3d4fa84-0760-49ee-8fd4-c819f9663f5c", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "IO balance has been sitting at 0% since roughly 16:20, about 40 minutes now, and the metric history since Nov 11 suggests the instance was probably stopped for a gap in that sample count. The vol-00612 queue depth is at 1.26 with a throttled baseline around 500 IOPS, which seems fine for now. Next I want to check for gaps in the alarm details and look at the three example alarms that have no actions configured.

There's also a system status checks alarm tied to i-1234567890abcdef0_5, an instance that's no longer in the current list and is sitting in INSUFFICIENT_DATA — likely deleted. None of the 12 active instances have StatusCheckFailed or BurstBalance alarms, and there's nothing for VolumeStalledIOCheck or volume status either. I should quickly check the thresholds on the three alarms currently in ALARM state plus the existing workshop ones, and then pull the full alarm details for those.

"}], "parent_id": "a8b60179-ad75-4091-a755-136e494e4f1e"}

{"id": "e1b2911e-3c1f-4206-9e1b-a6c4f7f300a0", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice the three workshop alarms have notifications disabled, and the EBS burst credit alarm is actually tied to a different instance than the one at 0%. That's enough investigation—time to write up the report, noting the alarm timestamps.

Data coverage for the eval volumes only goes back about 11 hours, and this whole account appears to be lab/evaluation resources rather than anything tagged as production, so I should frame the report accordingly and keep it concise.

"}], "parent_id": "a8b60179-ad75-4091-a755-136e494e4f1e"}

